WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.077
Here are 13 names.

00:00:02.077 --> 00:00:04.940
Which one appears most often?

00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:06.922
You could count by hand.

00:00:06.922 --> 00:00:08.690
Or you could keep a tally:

00:00:08.690 --> 00:00:12.090
one counter for each name, labelled with the name.

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:13.762
That's a dictionary.

00:00:13.762 --> 00:00:18.920
And to get the names out of a line of text in the first place, we use 'split'.

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:25.100
This is part 3 of Lecture 5: splitting strings, and counting with a dictionary.

00:00:25.867 --> 00:00:29.970
The string method 'split' breaks a string into a list of strings.

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:35.147
With no argument, it splits on whitespace, so we can think of the pieces as words.

00:00:35.497 --> 00:00:38.977
'Catch me if you can' becomes a list of 5 words.

00:00:39.327 --> 00:00:41.087
It's an ordinary list.

00:00:41.087 --> 00:00:46.287
Its length is 5, 'words[0]' is 'Catch', and you can loop over it.

00:00:47.067 --> 00:00:52.517
With no argument, any run of spaces counts as one separator.

00:00:52.517 --> 00:00:55.787
So, 'A lot of spaces' gives 4 words.

00:00:56.137 --> 00:01:03.289
But this line, 'first;second;third', with semicolons in between, has no spaces at all.

00:01:03.289 --> 00:01:05.617
'split' finds only one word.

00:01:05.967 --> 00:01:09.836
To split on another character, pass it as the delimiter.

00:01:09.836 --> 00:01:12.807
Split on a semicolon, and you get 3 pieces.

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:15.702
Here's a practice from the slides.

00:01:15.702 --> 00:01:17.680
An email header looks like this.

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:25.070
We want the domain of the address, UCT.EDU, and the month the email was sent, January.

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:28.077
First, split the line into words.

00:01:28.077 --> 00:01:31.860
The address is word 1, and the month is word 3.

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:38.770
Then split the address again, at the '@' sign, and take the part after it.

00:01:39.370 --> 00:01:44.559
This is the double split pattern: split a line one way to get its fields,

00:01:44.559 --> 00:01:46.905
then split one field another way.

00:01:46.905 --> 00:01:51.170
Dates, times and addresses are full of fields inside fields.

00:01:51.967 --> 00:01:53.423
Now, dictionaries.

00:01:53.423 --> 00:01:59.247
A list is a linear collection: values in order, found by their position.

00:01:59.597 --> 00:02:05.379
A dictionary is a bag of values, each with its own label, called its key.

00:02:05.379 --> 00:02:07.597
You find a value by its key.

00:02:07.947 --> 00:02:12.592
We make an empty dictionary, 'purse', and put values in by key:

00:02:12.592 --> 00:02:16.867
12 under 'money', 3 under 'candy', and 75 under 'tissues'.

00:02:17.217 --> 00:02:22.057
Looking up the key 'candy' in 'purse' gives its value: 3.

00:02:22.407 --> 00:02:26.982
Assigning to a key that's already there replaces its value.

00:02:26.982 --> 00:02:28.921
'Candy' goes from 3 to 5.

00:02:28.921 --> 00:02:31.247
Each key appears at most once.

00:02:31.597 --> 00:02:33.819
One correction to the slides.

00:02:33.819 --> 00:02:36.500
They say a dictionary has no order.

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:42.704
Since Python 3.7, a dictionary keeps its keys in the order they were first added.

00:02:42.704 --> 00:02:46.917
But you still look values up by key, never by position.

00:02:47.701 --> 00:02:52.501
A dictionary works like a list, except that it uses keys instead of positions.

00:02:52.851 --> 00:02:56.622
With a list, we append 21 and 183.

00:02:56.622 --> 00:03:00.171
Then we change position 0 to 23.

00:03:00.521 --> 00:03:06.295
With a dictionary, we store 21 under 'age', and 182 under 'course'.

00:03:06.295 --> 00:03:10.001
Then we change the value under 'age' to 23.

00:03:10.351 --> 00:03:14.889
So a list looks values up by position: 0, 1, 2, in order.

00:03:14.889 --> 00:03:18.791
A dictionary looks them up by any key you choose.

00:03:19.141 --> 00:03:24.985
You can also write a dictionary directly, with curly braces around key:value pairs.

00:03:24.985 --> 00:03:27.661
Empty braces make an empty dictionary.

00:03:28.434 --> 00:03:31.554
The most common use of a dictionary is counting.

00:03:31.904 --> 00:03:36.316
The first time we see a name, we store it with a count of 1.

00:03:36.316 --> 00:03:39.184
Each later time, we add 1 to its count.

00:03:39.534 --> 00:03:40.701
But be careful.

00:03:40.701 --> 00:03:45.214
Looking up a key that isn't there is an error: a KeyError.

00:03:45.564 --> 00:03:49.893
The 'in' operator tells us whether a key is in the dictionary.

00:03:49.893 --> 00:03:53.804
So, for each name, the loop asks: have I seen it before?

00:03:54.154 --> 00:03:56.719
Let's run it on 5 names.

00:03:56.719 --> 00:03:59.284
'C sev' is new: count 1.

00:03:59.284 --> 00:04:01.848
'C wen' is new: count 1.

00:04:01.848 --> 00:04:03.665
'C sev' again: 2.

00:04:03.665 --> 00:04:06.230
The next name is new: 1.

00:04:06.230 --> 00:04:08.474
And 'C wen' again: 2.

00:04:09.267 --> 00:04:11.304
Pause the video and predict.

00:04:11.304 --> 00:04:14.870
Run the same loop on the 13 names from the start.

00:04:14.870 --> 00:04:18.507
What's in 'counts', and which name is most common?

00:04:19.057 --> 00:04:20.484
'Zhen' wins, with 5.

00:04:20.484 --> 00:04:24.337
'C sev' and 'Marquard' have 3 each, and 'C wen' has 2.

00:04:24.337 --> 00:04:28.617
And the keys come out in the order each name was first seen.

00:04:29.401 --> 00:04:30.261
One trap.

00:04:30.261 --> 00:04:35.521
'in', on a dictionary, checks the keys, not the values.

00:04:35.871 --> 00:04:38.149
'Zhen' in 'counts' is True.

00:04:38.149 --> 00:04:43.212
But 5 in 'counts' is False, because 5 is a value, not a key.

00:04:43.212 --> 00:04:48.191
To check the values, ask whether 5 is in 'counts.values()'.

00:04:48.968 --> 00:04:51.530
Here's another practice from the slides.

00:04:51.530 --> 00:04:54.989
Keep asking the user for words until they type 'done'.

00:04:54.989 --> 00:04:58.448
For each word, say how many times it was typed before.

00:04:58.798 --> 00:05:03.340
If the word is already a key, print its count, then add 1 to it.

00:05:03.340 --> 00:05:07.598
If it isn't, the word is new, so store it with a count of 1.

00:05:07.948 --> 00:05:10.884
Type 'hi', 'bye', 'hi', 'hi'.

00:05:10.884 --> 00:05:13.111
The first 'hi' is new.

00:05:13.111 --> 00:05:19.388
The second was typed once before, and the third, twice before.

00:05:20.168 --> 00:05:24.974
Checking for a key, and using a default when it's missing, is so common

00:05:24.974 --> 00:05:28.088
that dictionaries have a method for it: 'get'.

00:05:28.438 --> 00:05:33.417
'get', with a key and a default, returns the value if the key is there,

00:05:33.417 --> 00:05:35.380
and the default if it isn't.

00:05:35.380 --> 00:05:39.238
It never raises a KeyError, and it doesn't add the key.

00:05:39.588 --> 00:05:45.588
With 'get', the four-line if/else of our counting loop becomes one line.

00:05:45.938 --> 00:05:50.197
'counts[name] = counts.get(name, 0) + 1'.

00:05:50.197 --> 00:05:57.365
Read it as: the count so far, or 0 if this is the first time, plus 1.

00:05:57.365 --> 00:06:00.378
This is the counting pattern.

00:06:00.978 --> 00:06:07.113
Put it together with 'split', and you can count every word in a line of text.

00:06:07.113 --> 00:06:08.866
'The' appears 4 times.

00:06:08.866 --> 00:06:11.018
'Ran' and 'car' twice each.

00:06:11.801 --> 00:06:15.908
Finally, a 'for' loop over a dictionary goes through its keys.

00:06:15.908 --> 00:06:18.161
Use each key to look up its value.

00:06:18.511 --> 00:06:25.140
The methods 'keys()', 'values()', and 'items()' give you the keys, the values, or both.

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:28.111
Each item is a pair, in round brackets.

00:06:28.461 --> 00:06:33.947
With two loop variables, the first gets the key, and the second gets the value.

00:06:33.947 --> 00:06:37.141
Those pairs are tuples, the subject of part 4.

00:06:37.934 --> 00:06:40.014
Let's sum up what we've seen.

00:06:40.364 --> 00:06:43.597
'split' turns a string into a list of words.

00:06:43.597 --> 00:06:47.124
The double split pattern splits one field again.

00:06:47.474 --> 00:06:50.322
A dictionary maps keys to values.

00:06:50.322 --> 00:06:56.794
A missing key is a KeyError, so check with 'in', or read safely with 'get'.

00:06:57.144 --> 00:07:02.504
To count anything, add 1 to 'counts.get(key, 0)'.

00:07:02.854 --> 00:07:04.153
Here's one to try.

00:07:04.153 --> 00:07:09.061
Write 'letter_counts', a function that counts each letter in a text,

00:07:09.061 --> 00:07:13.174
ignoring case, and skipping anything that isn't a letter.

00:07:13.724 --> 00:07:18.377
In part 4, we'll meet tuples, and use them to sort a dictionary

00:07:18.377 --> 00:07:21.404
and find the most common words in a text.

